  "query_preview": "..\n(Saturday through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1219 PM MDT Thu Oct 22 2026\n\nThe forecast for this weekend is trending even wetter across\ncentral and eastern NM due to the slow southerly track of the \n2nd upper low across AZ, eventually lifting northeast toward the \nFour Corners late Sunday. The latest NAM is notably slower to lift\nthe upper low out of south central AZ, so unlike the GFS it \nallows a backdoor front to slide into northeast NM late Saturday, \nwhich helps to force more precipitation across north central and \nnortheast areas through Sunday. Both PWATs and instability trend \ndown over the weekend relative to today/Friday, and so should the \nflood threat. However, we still may need a watch for the Ruidoso \narea for Sat/Sun due to burn scar sensitivity and possibly the \neastern plains due to the potential for training storms and \ninteraction with a potential backdoor cold front. Daytime \ntemperatures will trend down over the weekend and be below average\nareawide by Sunday. The upper low is forecast to eject east out \nof the southern Rockies on Monday, bringing some drying to much of\nthe area. However, sufficient PWATs and low level moisture \nconvergence will remain across the eastern plains for a few more \nshowers and storms. Some warming/drying is forecast early to mid  \nnext week behind the departing upper low, but there is some model \nspread of note. The subtropical jetstream is forecast to get \nactive over the region by late week, bringing back moisture and \nchances for showers and storms.",
